(2) The Router asks one folder name at a time:
```js
// core/Router/Router.js:103-104
for (const name of url.split("/").filter(Boolean)){
	page = await page.child(name);
```
child(name) tries jsonl first, then page.js:
```js
// core/Page/Page.class.js:367 and :385: a file line said "jsonl", or the loaded files.jsonl did
const page = await Page.jsonl(this.url + name + "/");
// :393: otherwise try page.js
const page = await Page.load(this.url + name + "/", 0);
```
Where the Page is created:
```js
// page.js: inside the child's own file, at import (Page.load only imports, :455)
export default new Page({ meta: import.meta, title: "Hello" });
// page.jsonl: core/Page/Jsonl.js:42, from line 1, then reader.attach(page) replays lines 2..n
const page = new Class(line, { url, jsonl_url: reader.url });
```
Either way the parent then calls this.add(name, page) (Page.class.js:319), which puts the child into parent.pages.

(3) A page.jsonl names its children with one line per child:
```jsonl
{"file":"notes/page.jsonl"}
```
```js
// core/Page/Listing.js:4, the pattern a file line must match to be a child
static CHILD = /^([^/]+)\/page\.(jsonl|js)$/;
// core/Page/Jsonl.js:83, 89: mark the kind, leave a stub that loads when visited
(this.page.child_kinds ??= new Map()).set(dir, kind);
if (!this.page.children.has(dir)) this.page.children.set(dir, null);
```
No page.jsonl uses a "children" string (0 of 921).

